Follow-up to aiecompiler_part_strix_confirmed.log. That log proved the
--part mechanism works when fed a real string (Strix's, pulled from the one
xclbin this install ships). This log looks for the Phoenix equivalent, which
that xclbin database does NOT contain (data/parts/xilinx/xclbin/ has only a
strx/ subdirectory, no phx/ or equivalent).

Rather than guess, byte-scanned the actual payload DLL that implements
architecture derivation (found by grepping every .dll/.exe under this env
for the literal error string "could not be auto derived" --
lib/win64.o/aiecompiler_client.dll, 132 MB, is the one that matters; the
256 KB aiecompiler.exe is a thin dispatcher). Two things came out of that
scan, both recorded in notes_aiecompiler_part_db.log:

1. Mangled C++ symbols in BuildDevice: isPHXPart, isSTRXPart, isAie2pPart,
   isAie4Part, isAieMlPart, etc. -- "PHX" is a real, code-level device
   family this compiler recognizes, distinct from and alongside "STRX".
   A live diagnostic string confirms it's reachable code, not dead:
   "...if not multi-layer and the part is not PHX/STRX)".
2. A table of real part-name strings: xc10AIE2P_ML-die-0x-e-S-es1 (Strix,
   already confirmed working), xc10AIE24x5-die-1LP-e-S-es1, xc10AIE2H,
   xc10MDS1-die-0x-e-S-es1, xc10SWV1H, xc10Turin-die-0x-e-S-es1, plus
   several Versal xc2ve33xx/35xx/38xx/58xx parts. MDS1/SWV1 match the
   "mds"/"swv" device names already ruled out as pre-silicon test hardware
   in the aie4_models dead end (see notes_aie2_device_dtypes.log) --
   consistent, not coincidental: this table spans many AMD chips, most
   unrelated to this project.

"AIE24x5" stood out because of an existing finding in docs/DECISIONS.md:
`xrt-smi examine -r platform` reports Total Columns: 5 on this exact
Phoenix desktop, not the 4 every existing tool assumes -- i.e. this chip's
physical AIE array really is a 4-row x 5-column layout, of which this
project's own xclbins expose only a 4x4 subset. "AIE24x5" reads literally
as "AIE2 architecture, 4x5 array" and matches that measurement, not just
the name.

Tried it two ways. First, the bare architecture-family string:

  --part=xc10AIE24x5

Result: a NEW warning appears -- "Partial part name is not supported,
Please provide the full part name (Eg. xcvc1902-vsva2197-2MP-e-S)" -- but
the compiler proceeds anyway, derives __AIE_ARCH__=20 (Strix derived 21;
different generation number, as expected for a different chip), and reaches
the same downstream failure every part-string success now reaches (missing
<iostream> -- see aiecompiler_hostlib_missing.log). The "auto derived"
error that blocked every previous attempt is gone.

Second, the full part name from the same string table (die/package suffix
included, matching the pattern the warning asked for):

  --part=xc10AIE24x5-die-1LP-e-S-es1

Result: no warning, clean derivation, __AIE_ARCH__=20, same downstream wall.

Neither run reaches "architecture could not be auto derived." This is the
strongest evidence available on this machine that xc10AIE24x5-die-1LP-e-S-es1
is Phoenix's real device-model string in this compiler's part database --
not confirmed against an AMD-published part list (none was found anywhere
in this SDK), but derived from the compiler's own code, corroborated by an
independent on-hardware measurement (xrt-smi's column count) that predates
this investigation and was not chosen to fit it.

